content.text:
Hmm, my transaction loop sets failureReason on ALL cancelled payments (including previously cancelled ones) — overwriting history. Simplify: drop that inner loop; instead update PENDING normally and AUTHORIZED only if release failed:

Actually simpler and history-safe:

```ts
const rows = await tx.payment.findMany({ where: { contractId: contract.id, status: { in: ["PENDING","AUTHORIZED"] } }, select: { id: true, status: true } });
for (const row of rows) {
  await tx.payment.update({ where: { id: row.id }, data: { status: "CANCELLED", failureReason: row.status === "AUTHORIZED" ? `HOLD_CANCELLED_UNRELEASED: ${params.reason ?? "case-cancelled"}` : params.reason ?? "CASE_CANCELLED" } });
}
```

Wait — release actually handles AUTHORIZED payments through the provider before the transaction; leftovers only when real provider. In sandbox the release succeeded, so AUTHORIZED set is empty locally. But if release threw? It catches — local update proceeds as fallback. But careful: if release succeeded, provider event already set them CANCELLED, so `status in [PENDING,AUTHORIZED]` finds none. Good.

Also guest cancel (cancelGuestCase, guest/service.ts:599) — does it cancel contract + payments? Check it uses cancelContract. Let me refactor the block I just added.

